{"page": 25, "text": "During the three months ended September 30, 2026, the Group recognized impairment charges of $873 million related to Brazil within the International segment, due to the previously disclosed challenging trading environment driven by Government implemented socio-economic measures together with the Measure announced on September 25, 2026 banning fixed-odds sports betting and online gaming. Out of the total impairment charge of $873 million, $296 million is related to long-lived assets and $541 million is related to goodwill. The long-lived asset impairment charges primarily included $122 million related to the trademark, $108 million related to customer relationships, $31 million related to technology, and $22 million related to capitalized development costs. The impairment charges related to trademark and customer relationships were recognized in sales and marketing expenses; technology and capitalized development costs in technology, research and development expenses; license cost in cost of sales. The impairment charges related to property, plant and equipment and right-of-use assets were recognized in general and administrative expenses.\nDuring the three months ended September 30, 2025, the Group recognized impairment charges of $559 million mainly related to Junglee. The Promotion and Regulation of Online Gaming Act, 2025 (the \u201cAct\u201d), which was passed by the Indian Parliament and received Presidential assent on August 22, 2025, banned all forms of online real money gaming in India. As a result of the Act, from August 22, 2025, Junglee Games Inc (\u201cJunglee,\u201d \u201cJunglee Games\u201d) ceased offering all real-money games in India. The Junglee impairment charge is $556 million before income taxes. The assets impaired substantially consists of goodwill of $517 million, acquired and developed intangibles of $32 million and other long-lived assets of $7 million. The $517 million of goodwill impaired is not deductible for tax purposes, and therefore there is no income tax benefit. Income tax impacts arising for acquired and developed intangibles and other long-lived assets are not material.\n22\nAdjusted net income attributable to Flutter shareholders\nSee below a reconciliation of Adjusted net income attributable to Flutter shareholders to net income (loss), the most comparable GAAP measure.\nThree months ended\nSeptember 30,\n($ in millions) 2026 2025\nNet loss\n(1,148 ) (789 )\nAdd (Less):\nTransaction fees and associated costs\n1 204\nRestructuring and integration costs\n108 59\nImpairment\n873 559\nLegal loss contingencies\n105 \u2014\nAmortization of acquired intangibles\n222 235\nShare-based compensation\n59 71\nLoss on settlement of long-term debt\n\u2014 9\nFinancing related fees not eligible for capitalization\n\u2014 2\nFair value (gain) loss on derivative instruments\n4 (11 )\nFair value (gain) loss on Fox Option Liability\n(122 ) (126 )\nFair value (gain) loss on Investment\n1 \u2014\nTax impact of above adjustments1\n(148 ) (26 )\nAdjusted net (loss) income\n(45 ) 187\nLess:\n"}
